+# Daily Grind WebMCP
+
+The static homepage loads `webmcp.js` and exposes these client-side tools in a WebMCP-capable browser:
+
+| Tool | Example arguments | Result |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `get_seasonal_favorites` | `{}` or `{"query":"oat"}` | Displayed drink IDs, names, descriptions, and detail links; no page changes. |
+| `order_drink` | `{"drinkId":"maple-oat-latte","quantity":2}` | Adds to the visible order draft and returns its items. Quantity defaults to one. |
+| `highlight_drink` | `{"drinkId":"cold-brew"}` | Highlights and focuses the card, scrolls it into view, and selects it in the form. |
+
+Drink IDs are `maple-oat-latte`, `dark-roast-espresso`, and `cold-brew`. Search matches all supplied words against names and descriptions, ignoring case. Invalid arguments return actionable error objects without changing the draft. Each draft holds at most ten of each drink.
+
+Orders are in-memory drafts for the current tab only. They reset on reload, never reach a backend, and do not take payment. Visitors can use the same order actions through the card buttons and order form, and clear the draft with the Clear order button.
+
+The implementation prefers `document.modelContext`, falls back to `navigator.modelContext` for older preview browsers, and skips registration when neither API exists. Native WebMCP requires a supporting browser with the experimental feature enabled and a secure context (HTTPS or localhost). Registration uses abort signals for page lifecycle cleanup and re-registers after a back/forward cache restoration. No backend MCP transport, API keys, dependencies, or server entrypoints are involved.
